## Pattern 153: Marketplace Source-Type Polymorphism (SHA-Pinned)

**Prevalence:** Multi-source: 100+ entries in `/tmp/cpo/.claude-plugin/marketplace.json` and similar registries
**Related patterns:** [40-Point Skill Security Checklist](/prompt-context-patterns/catalog/categories/patterns-advanced-safety#pattern-67-40-point-security-skill-review-checklist), [Policy-as-Data](/prompt-context-patterns/catalog/categories/patterns-advanced-safety#pattern-69-policy-as-data-declarative-yaml-configs)

**What it is:** Plugin/skill marketplaces accept multiple source types (git, github, http, local) with the type-specific fields required to make each install *reproducible and verifiable* — most importantly, a SHA pin for any git/github source. Supply-chain anchor at the registry layer.

### Positive Example

```json
{
  "plugins": [
    {
      "name": "pr-review-toolkit",
      "source": {
        "type": "github",
        "owner": "acme",
        "repo": "pr-review-toolkit",
        "ref": "v1.4.2",
        "sha": "c3a7e4b1f8d2..."
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "internal-deploy",
      "source": {
        "type": "git",
        "url": "git@git.internal:tools/deploy.git",
        "ref": "main",
        "sha": "9f2b1e0..."
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "docs-fetcher",
      "source": {
        "type": "http",
        "url": "https://internal.example/skills/docs-fetcher-1.2.0.tar.gz",
        "sha256": "5b7c..."
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**Rules:**
- Any `git`/`github` source MUST include a `sha` — `ref` alone is mutable.
- `http` sources MUST include a `sha256`.
- A skill upgrade is the act of replacing the SHA, not editing in-place.

**Why this works:** Mutable refs (`main`, `v1.4.2` tag) can be force-pushed. A SHA pin means the install is deterministic — every consumer gets identical bytes. The polymorphic source schema lets registries accept internal and public sources side-by-side without losing the integrity guarantee.

### Negative Example

```json
{
  "plugins": [
    { "name": "pr-review-toolkit", "url": "github.com/acme/pr-review-toolkit" }
  ]
}
```

**Why this fails:** No SHA pin — the maintainer (or an attacker who compromises the repo) can change what `main` points to silently. No source-type polymorphism — internal git and HTTP archives can't be expressed. No integrity field for archives — a tampered tarball installs with no warning.

